# Syntax: docs/LANGUAGE.md. Check with `rbb bid check`.
#
# When the auction itself forces to game (Rick, 2026-09-30): "we should
# have a state 'in game force' that 2/1 triggers, or 4th suit forcing,
# or XYZ 2D, etc." The state is `we.forcing = game`. A rule whose call
# creates it says so with `sets forcing=game` (the two-over-one on a 2/1
# card, a strong jump shift); the declarations here are the forces that
# belong to the shape of the auction rather than to one rule's meaning,
# and apply whichever rule made or explains the call.

module game-force "When the auction forces to game"
  kind   system
  needs  base
  skill  bidding_conventions/two_over_one
  param  wolff = bidding_conventions/wolff_signoff default false

# Responder's third bid at the three level, below game: "a 3rd bid by
# responder at the 3 level or higher is usually considered a GF" (Rick).
# 3NT and higher are game themselves. Three of a suit already agreed is
# a sign-off, not a force: 1♠-2♠-2NT-3♣-3♦-3♠ declines a game try
# (majors/game-tries.bid).
force game when partner.opened, !they.bid, me.bids = 2, call >= 3C, call <= 3S, !(we.trump is suit, call = 3{trump})

# After opener's 2NT rebid (18-19), anything but pass forces to game,
# except Wolff's 3C, which is there to stop in a part score.
force game after 1x (P) 1y (P) 2N (P) when !call = P, !(wolff, call = 3C)

# After 1M-1NT-2NT too (Rick, 2026-11-27: "after a 1M-1N-2N, any bid
# should be GF"): the forcing 1NT is 6-12 opposite 18-19. The pattern
# above is for a one-level suit response.
force game after 1M (P) 1N (P) 2N (P) when !call = P

# ── In a game force with nothing else to say: bid game ───────────────
# A game force forbids the pass, but when no rule applies the engine
# passes anyway (docs/CONTRACT.md, "No rule"). Brill dailies, round 3:
# 2C P 2D P 3C P 4C P passed (2026-11-24 MP-strong b5), and the passes
# after 1M-1NT-2NT. These are the last resort, below every other rule (priority -70 and
# lower; the pack's own fallbacks go down to -60):
# the cheapest sensible game, a known fit first, then notrump, then my
# own long suit. Uncontested only: over their bid a pass may be a
# forcing pass, and pulling their 4S or our double lost (random deals).
# Each is a game, so they end the force: two calls no
# rule reads cannot carry the auction past game this way (Rick,
# 2026-11-27: a "never pass an unknown call" rule could run to 7NT).
when we.gf, !game_reached, !they.bid
  4S  "Game in the spade fit"              shows we.fit(S).min >= 8
      priority -70
  4H  "Game in the heart fit"              shows we.fit(H).min >= 8, we.fit(S).min <= 7
      priority -70
  3N  "Game in notrump"                    shows we.fit(S).min <= 7, we.fit(H).min <= 7
      priority -71
  5C  "Game in the club fit"               shows we.fit(C).min >= 8, we.fit(S).min <= 7, we.fit(H).min <= 7
      priority -71
  5D  "Game in the diamond fit"            shows we.fit(D).min >= 8, we.fit(S).min <= 7, we.fit(H).min <= 7
      priority -71
  4S  "Game: five or more spades"          shows S>=5
      priority -72
  4H  "Game: five or more hearts"          shows H>=5
      priority -72
  5C  "Game: five or more clubs"           shows C>=5
      priority -73
  5D  "Game: five or more diamonds"        shows D>=5
      priority -73
  4S  "Game: four spades"                  shows S>=4
      priority -74
  4H  "Game: four hearts"                  shows H>=4
      priority -74
  3N  "Game"                               priority -75
  4H  "Game"                               priority -76
  4S  "Game"                               priority -76
  5C  "Game"                               priority -77
  5D  "Game"                               priority -77
